{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/4ze94zi5","slideIndex":0,"position":{"left":652,"top":473,"width":260,"height":26},"text":"CPU usage","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":17,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/y90nupkv","slideIndex":0,"text":"Explain the setup as an intermediary: a visitor’s request passes through Cloudflare before reaching the site. A 502 error means that intermediary could not obtain the response it needed. The 82% figure describes Cloudflare’s traffic drop, not 82% of the Internet.\nAn everyday example: a visitor may be unable to open a store or complete an online task. This is an illustration of availability impact, not a measured financial-loss claim.\nSources: https://blog.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-outage/\nhttps://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/\n\nPresentation detail:\nCloudflare sits between visitors and websites. Its servers deliver content and screen requests before they reach a website’s own server.\nOn July 2, a firewall rule update overloaded servers worldwide. Visitors saw “502 Bad Gateway” errors instead of web pages.\nThe outage lasted 27 minutes. CPU usage reached 100%, and Cloudflare’s traffic fell 82% at its worst.\nWhy it mattered: one shared provider’s failure interrupted many customers at once, even when their own websites were otherwise healthy."}
{"kind":"slide","id":"sl/hwbqtkby","slideIndex":1,"layout":{"id":"ly/ov6t8ra5","name":"BLANK"},"title":"Why a text-search rule consumed the CPU","textShapes":14,"backgroundColor":"rgba(19,32,52,1)"}
{"kind":"shape","id":"sh/i14jex0z","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":0,"top":0,"width":9.36,"height":540},"name":"Google Shape;250;p34","geometry":"rect","fillColor":"#189D94","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":18.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#000000","alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/3md0n21k","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":54,"top":37,"width":860,"height":54},"text":"Why a text-search rule consumed the CPU","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":30.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/4nm1g7i5","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":56,"top":507,"width":250,"height":18},"text":"CLOUDFLARE 2019","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":10.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#9DACBC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/5ovipszq","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":859,"top":507,"width":60,"height":18},"text":"2 / 5","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":10.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#9DACBC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/uxkzad03","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":56,"top":120,"width":360,"height":25},"text":"THE SIMPLIFIED PATTERN","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":14,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#189D94","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/vyt0ji1o","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":56,"top":166,"width":355,"height":66},"text":".*.*=.*","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":50,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#189D94","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/gz2hcni9","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":56,"top":252,"width":350,"height":144},"text":"Each .* can match almost any amount of text. The overlapping sections create many possible ways to split the same input.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":22.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/h0bilsju","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":56,"top":422,"width":350,"height":52},"text":"A regex is a pattern for searching text.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#9DACBC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/etojmh07","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":119,"width":435,"height":34},"text":"01  Inspect the request","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/fux0vmhs","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":158,"width":435,"height":65},"text":"The web application firewall (WAF) ran this rule to detect malicious JavaScript.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/bypg7eh4","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":226,"width":435,"height":34},"text":"02  Retry possible matches","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/axgfe9gz","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":265,"width":435,"height":65},"text":"Backtracking tries alternative matches. Too many retries use up processing time.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/x07y94za","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":333,"width":435,"height":34},"text":"03  Crowd out normal work","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/czyxgjyp","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":372,"width":435,"height":65},"text":"The check consumed CPU that servers needed to respond to visitors.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/j29gbehg","slideIndex":1,"position":{"left":470,"top":447,"width":435,"height":52},"text":"“Simulate” mode still runs the check. It only prevents the rule from blocking visitors.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":18,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#9DACBC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/hwbqtkby","slideIndex":1,"text":"Read the pattern aloud as: any text, followed by any text, followed by an equals sign, then any text. This is a simplified fragment, not the complete deployed expression.\nIllustrative example: when a candidate input lacks an equals sign, a backtracking matcher can repeatedly redistribute characters between the wildcard sections before deciding there is no match. A check can be logically correct yet too expensive to run on every request. Avoid calling this an infinite loop: the problem is excessive repeated work.\nSources: https://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/\nhttps://blog.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-outage/\n\nPresentation detail:\nThe web application firewall (WAF) checks requests for suspicious content. This update targeted malicious JavaScript.\nA regular expression, or regex, is a text-search pattern. The simplified fragment .*.*=.* contains overlapping “match anything” sections.\nBacktracking means retrying different ways to match the same text. Here, the repeated work consumed the CPU needed to serve requests.\n“Simulate” mode only stopped the rule from blocking visitors. It still ran the expensive check on real traffic."}
