{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/t8725sry","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":135,"top":122,"width":265,"height":60},"text":"CPU protection","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/s7ylcnqd","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":430,"top":120,"width":475,"height":74},"text":"An earlier performance refactor accidentally removed a safeguard against costly regex processing.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21.51,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/76pk329s","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":56,"top":208,"width":63,"height":48},"text":"02","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":34,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#189D94","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/65w3ax87","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":135,"top":210,"width":265,"height":60},"text":"Performance tests","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/hc729cru","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":430,"top":208,"width":475,"height":74},"text":"Tests checked allow/block decisions, but did not catch runaway CPU use. Correct answers were not enough.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21.51,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/gbylg7q9","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":56,"top":296,"width":63,"height":48},"text":"03","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":34,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#189D94","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/fapk729o","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":135,"top":298,"width":265,"height":60},"text":"A limited rollout","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/u9g3ex83","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":430,"top":296,"width":475,"height":74},"text":"The rule reached the world without a small trial first. One defect affected many locations together.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21.51,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/xgn2x8rq","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":56,"top":384,"width":63,"height":48},"text":"04","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":34,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#189D94","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/wfe143q5","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":135,"top":386,"width":265,"height":60},"text":"Independent recovery","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/ozap4bil","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":430,"top":384,"width":475,"height":74},"text":"The outage also blocked internal tools, making emergency access and recovery harder.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":21.51,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/p036xgz6","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":135,"top":478,"width":770,"height":25},"text":"The rule triggered the incident. Existing weaknesses made it global and harder to stop.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#9DACBC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/ofy9wn61","slideIndex":2,"text":"Case analysis: several defenses failed together. A useful test plan asks both “Does this rule classify traffic correctly?” and “Can we afford to execute it under load?”\nA staged rollout exposes a small traffic sample first. Teams watch errors and CPU before expanding it. That limits how many customers encounter an undetected defect.\nThe debt connection is the accumulated risk in testing, deployment, and recovery procedures. The new regex triggered the incident, while existing weaknesses increased its severity.\nSource: https://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/\n\nPresentation detail:\nEarlier refactoring accidentally removed a CPU safeguard. The effort to improve performance weakened a protection against costly rules.\nTests checked which requests to allow or block. They did not catch runaway CPU use, so passing tests gave incomplete reassurance.\nThe rollout sent the rule worldwide without a limited trial. A local defect therefore became a global availability problem.\nRecovery also depended on affected systems. Engineers struggled to access internal tools, making an emergency change harder."}
