{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/jyx0ra1s","slideIndex":3,"text":"All times refer to July 2, 2019, in UTC. A rollback restores an earlier configuration or version. Disabling the affected component first removes the immediate load, giving the team room to investigate.\nMake the two recovery milestones explicit: people could access websites again at 14:09, but the WAF returned at 14:52. Traffic recovery therefore does not mean every protection was already back. The detailed postmortem gives the 14:07 termination time and 14:00 diagnosis.\nSources: https://blog.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-outage/\nhttps://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/\n\nPresentation detail:\n13:42 UTC: the rule goes live. By 14:00, engineers identify the WAF as the source of CPU exhaustion.\n14:07 UTC: they disable the WAF globally. By 14:09, CPU usage and traffic return to normal.\nThey roll back the faulty rule and test the fix. At 14:52 UTC, they restore the WAF worldwide.\nThe tradeoff: websites recover before WAF inspection returns. Other security protections remain active during that interval.\nImage: original CPU chart, Cloudflare engineering postmortem. https://blog.cloudflare.com/_emdash/api/media/file/01KW44G5FHMGT7B6C2NGNFWVW7.png\nThe chart shows one location, not a separately measured global average."}
