{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/pwretofm","slideIndex":4,"position":{"left":492,"top":402,"width":413,"height":79},"text":"Preserve safety limits as well as feature behavior. Test resource use under load.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":22.67,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#F7F9FC","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/i107q5of","slideIndex":4,"text":"The first bullet describes work reported complete. The following actions were announced in the July 12 postmortem, so this slide does not claim they had all shipped by that date.\nRE2 explanation: for a fixed pattern, work scales proportionally with input length instead of exploding through repeated retries. This does not mean every expression takes the same time. RE2 also restricts syntax: features such as backreferences and look-around are unsupported. A migration therefore needs compatibility tests.\nSources: https://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/\nhttps://github.com/google/re2\n\nPresentation detail:\nCompleted by July 12: restored the CPU safeguard and inspected all 3,868 managed rules for similar problems.\nPlanned testing: profile each rule’s performance. That means measuring its processing cost, beyond whether it gives the right answer.\nPlanned rollout: release rules in stages. Planned engine change: use RE2 or Rust regex with runtime guarantees.\nWhy the engine matters: RE2 bounds matching time linearly in input length for a fixed pattern, avoiding unbounded backtracking.\n\nAdditional lessons and discussion:\nThese are the presentation’s conclusions from the case. Corrective maintenance addresses an existing defect. Preventive maintenance reduces the likelihood of future failures.\nRefactoring changes a program’s internal structure while preserving external behavior. The case shows why a team must identify the protections that need to survive a code change. A proposed regression test could run difficult inputs and fail when a rule exceeds an agreed processing budget. This is a recommendation, not a claim about Cloudflare’s exact implementation.\nDiscussion question: If a change returns the same answers but makes the service unavailable under load, has it preserved the behavior users need?\nConcept source: https://refactoring.com/\nCase evidence: https://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/\nMaintenance includes keeping changes safe. Fixing the broken rule restores service, while stronger tests help prevent another failure.\nTechnical debt can live in processes too. Saving time on rollout or testing can create larger debugging and outage costs later.\nRefactoring should preserve behavior. Teams also need regression tests for safety limits that ordinary feature tests may miss.\nPractical takeaway: test correctness and resource use, expand releases gradually, and rehearse recovery when normal tools are unavailable."}